# Developing {{ app_slug }}

A **tenant** of a shared k3s cluster. Flux owns everything under
`kubernetes/flux/`: edit YAML, open a {{ change_request }}, and on merge to
`main` the operator-side Flux `Kustomization` reconciles this repo into
`{{ app_namespace }}`. There is no `kubectl apply` / `helm upgrade` in the normal
flow.

## Golden rules

- **Never `kubectl apply` or `helm upgrade` to deploy.** Flux reverts drift.
  Preview with `kustomize build kubernetes/flux`; deploy by merging.
- **Never push to `main`.** Branch → {{ change_request }} → merge.
- **Never commit secrets.**
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %} Values live in the `{{ secrets_backend }}`
  backend; `kubernetes/flux/externalsecret.yaml` is the only reference this repo
  holds.
{%- endif %}
- **Stay inside `{{ app_namespace }}`.** The Flux service account is RBAC-scoped
  to it.
- **Structural changes come from the template.** This repo was generated by
  copier; enabling a component (HPA, internal route, metrics, pull credential)
  is `copier update` with the answer flipped, which renders the manifest AND its
  kustomization entry together. Hand-assembly is how a half-enabled component
  happens.

## Local loop

```bash
task lint                 # every gate that runs locally
task render               # exactly what Flux will apply
{% if enable_image_build %}task build                # docker build
{% endif %}```

Secret detection is {% if ci_shape == 'none' %}pre-commit only{% else %}pipeline-only; `pre-commit install` covers it locally{% endif %}.
Fix lint before opening the {{ change_request }}.

## Deploy + verify

Merging to `main` triggers a reconcile (a short git poll is the fallback). With
a read-only kubeconfig from the operator on `KUBECONFIG`:

```bash
task flux:status          # flux get kustomization {{ app_slug }}
task flux:reconcile       # force an immediate reconcile
{% if secrets_backend != 'none' %}task secrets:check        # ExternalSecret sync state
{% endif %}kubectl get pods -n {{ app_namespace }}
```

If a change did not take: check the Kustomization is `Ready`, then the workload.
The usual causes are a failing `kustomize build` (CI would have caught it), an
`ExternalSecret` waiting on a store the operator has not wired yet, or an image
tag that does not exist in the registry.

## Conventions already wired in `kubernetes/flux/`

The full account is [`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`](../../../docs/ARCHITECTURE.md); the
short version, for editing:

- **Routing.** Public `{{ app_slug }}.{{ external_domain }}` is self-serve — the
  IngressRoute's `external-dns` target annotation provisions the record.
  Platform middlewares are referenced cross-namespace from `traefik`.
- **TLS.** A per-host `Certificate` issues into this namespace; Traefik reads the
  Secret only from the route's own namespace.
{%- if secrets_backend != 'none' %}
- **Secrets.** `ExternalSecret` → `ClusterSecretStore`
  `{{ secrets_backend }}-{{ app_slug }}` (operator-owned, scoped to this
  namespace).
{%- if secrets_backend == 'onepassword' %} `remoteRef.key` is the prefixed item
  title `"{{ app_slug }}: <Item>"`, `remoteRef.property` the field.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
- **Alerts.** Both expressions pin `namespace` and `deployment`: tenant rules are
  evaluated cluster-wide, so an unpinned `absent()` never fires once any
  namespace has a like-named Deployment.
- **Hardening.** Non-root, read-only root filesystem, dropped capabilities, and
  soft avoidance of the storage node.

